edia/divine-comedy"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/divine-comedy.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Divine Comedy</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Divine Comedy is a long narrative poem by Dante Alighieri, written in the Florentine vernacular from about 1308 until his death in 1321.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/divine-kingship"><span class="enc-name">Divine kingship</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Divine kingship is the idea that a ruler is a god or possesses divine status.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/division-of-leipzig"><span class="enc-name">Division of Leipzig</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Division of Leipzig of 1485 split the lands of the house of Wettin between the brothers Ernest and Albert.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/divrigi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/divrigi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Divriği</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Divriği is a town in Sivas province in eastern Turkey, near the upper Euphrates.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/divrigi-great-mosque-and-hospital"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/divrigi-great-mosque-and-hospital.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Divriği Great Mosque and Hospital</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Divriği Great Mosque and Hospital is a joined mosque and hospital in eastern Anatolia, built in 1228–1229 by the Mengujekid ruler Ahmad Shah and his wife Turan Malik.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/divya-upadesh"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/divya-upadesh.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Divya Upadesh</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Divya Upadesh (&quot;Divine Counsel&quot;) is the set of final political teachings of Prithvi Narayan Shah, the founder of unified Nepal, delivered to his courtiers at…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/diwan"><span class="enc-name">Diwan</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The diwan was a register and administrative office in Islamic states.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/diwan-dina-nath"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/diwan-dina-nath.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Diwan Dina Nath</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Diwan Dina Nath was a senior official of the Sikh Empire who managed its financial administration, succeeding Diwan Mohkam Chand.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/diwan-lughat-al-turk"><span class="enc-name">Diwan Lughat al-Turk</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Diwan Lughat al-Turk ('Compendium of the Languages of the Turks') is a dictionary of the Turkic languages written in Arabic by Mahmud al-Kashgari, a member of the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/diwan-mohkam-chand"><span class="enc-name">Diwan Mohkam Chand</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Diwan Mohkam Chand was a leading official and military commander of the Sikh Empire under Ranjit Singh.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/diwanus"><span class="enc-name">Diwanus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Diwanus was a leader of the Bartians, one of the Old Prussian peoples, during the Great Prussian Uprising against the Teutonic Order that began in 1260.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/diyala-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/diyala-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Diyala River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Diyala is a river rising in the Zagros Mountains of western Iran, where it is called the Sirwan, and flowing through north-eastern Iraq to join the Tigris just…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/diyar-bakr"><span class="enc-name">Diyar Bakr</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Diyar Bakr was the medieval Arabic name for the northernmost province of the Jazira, the upper Tigris basin around Amid (modern Diyarbakır) and Mayyafariqin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/diyar-mudar"><span class="enc-name">Diyar Mudar</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Diyar Mudar is the medieval Arabic name for the western part of the Jazira, the upper Mesopotamian region along the Euphrates and the Balikh, named after the Mudar…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/diyar-rabia"><span class="enc-name">Diyar Rabi'a</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Diyar Rabi'a, 'the abode of the Rabi'a', was the eastern province of the Jazira in upper Mesopotamia in early Islamic times, around Mosul, Nisibis and the upper Tigris.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/diyarbakir"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/diyarbakir.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Diyarbakir</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Diyarbakir, the ancient Amida, is a city in south-eastern Anatolia on the upper Tigris.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/dizak"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/dizak.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Dizak</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Dizak was one of the five Armenian melikdoms of Karabakh, known as the Khamsa, in the south of the region.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/djanet"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/djanet.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Djanet</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Djanet is an oasis town in south-eastern Algeria, at the foot of the Tassili n'Ajjer plateau.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/djedars"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/djedars.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Djedars</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Djedars are thirteen stepped pyramid-shaped mausoleums on hilltops near Frenda and Tiaret in north-western Algeria, built between the 5th and 7th centuries.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/djehutyhotep"><span class="enc-name">Djehutyhotep</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Djehutyhotep was a nomarch of the Hare nome in Middle Egypt under the Twelfth Dynasty kings Amenemhat II, Senusret II and Senusret III.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/djemal-pasha"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/djemal-pasha.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Djemal Pasha</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Djemal Pasha was an Ottoman officer and politician, one of the three Young Turk leaders who governed the empire during the First World War.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/djenne"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/djenne.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Djenné</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Djenné is a city in the inland Niger Delta of present-day Mali, famous for its mud-brick Great Mosque.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/djerba"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/djerba.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Djerba</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Djerba is an island in the Gulf of Gabès off southern Tunisia, the largest in North Africa.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/djibo-bakary"><span class="enc-name">Djibo Bakary</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Djibo Bakary was a Nigerien trade unionist and politician who led the Sawaba party.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/djinguereber-mosque"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/djinguereber-mosque.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Djinguereber Mosque</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Djinguereber Mosque is the great mosque of Timbuktu in Mali, a landmark of earthen Sudano-Sahelian architecture.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/djoser"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/djoser.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Djoser</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Djoser was a king of ancient Egypt's Third Dynasty who reigned in the 27th century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/djoumbe-fatima"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/djoumbe-fatima.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Djoumbe Fatima</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Djoumbe Fatima was queen of Mohéli in the Comoros, succeeding her father Ramanetaka, a Merina prince, as a child in 1842.</span></a></li><li class=